  ifdp.push_integral_in_range(1618230402i64, 946684801i64, 4133980799i64, );
  ifdp.push_str_u8(&[0x0,0x0,0x0,0x0,0x1,0x0,0x0,0x40,0x0,0x95,0x0,0x1,0x0,0x40,0x1,0x0,0x0,0xc0,0x73,0x65,0x72,0x63,0x27,0x8,0x46,0xd7,0xfd,0xfd,0x50,0xfd,0x78,0x6e,0x0,]); // (len=33), Limit: 4000000; not terminated!
  consider ifdp.push_str_u8_no_term instead?







  Use the inverse fdp, compile and run it, to reach the prior line, then smartly modify it, to reach the goal line.



  Don't modify the fuzz target itself. However, you can add as many prints (std::cout) as you like. For example, if you think it would be useful to print the block header hashes, to use them in the inverse FDP, you may do so.
  You can also print the filter hashes, etc ...

```

</details>

Maybe if someone is bored they can generalize it, so that the LLM decides itself where to start and what coverage to add, but this seems too open ended for me to work on.

